    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Tekton is a family-owned company in Grand Rapids, Michigan, but most of its tools are not made there. By the company's own published count, about 82 percent of the current lineup comes from Taiwan and 12 percent from the United States, with the rest from Germany, Canada and a few European countries. None of that is hidden: every product page lists the country of origin. What actually protects you as a buyer is not the flag on the tool but the warranty behind it, and Tekton's is one of the simplest in the business.
        </p>

        <div id="who-is-tekton" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who Tekton Is and How It Sells
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton describes itself as a family-owned hand tool company based in Grand Rapids, Michigan. Its headquarters, warehouse and support staff sit on Roger B. Chaffee SE on the south side of the city, and the phone support line is staffed from there on weekdays. The family behind it is the Amash family. Press profiles, including one from SlashGear that draws on Tekton's own videos, trace the business back to the 1960s and a founder, Attallah Amash, who started out selling door to door. The company is run today by his son John Amash as CEO, with his brother Jeff alongside him.
          </p>

          <p>
            For much of its history the business sold tools to other businesses rather than to the public, and it has also traded under the name Michigan Industrial Tools. The shift to a direct-to-consumer hand tool brand under the Tekton name came after the second generation joined in the late 2000s. That is why you will see some reviews date Tekton to around 2009 while the company itself is decades older. Both are true, depending on whether you mean the brand you buy today or the family business underneath it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tekton sells mainly through its own website, Tekton.com, where orders typically ship the same day, shipping is free over $25, and purchases earn 10 percent back in store credit. It also sells through a limited number of authorized dealers, and Amazon carries genuine Tekton tools. Tekton's own FAQ is candid about the catch: other sellers may be offering an older model, and some marketplace sellers list used or fake Tekton tools. The company also says plainly that it typically does not run sales, so the price you see is close to the price you will pay.
          </p>

          <p>
            The product philosophy matters for the origin question. Tekton runs a single line of tools arranged by size and type, builds its sets out of the same individual components it sells singly, and avoids skipping sizes. In practice that means the 10 mm socket you replace after losing one is the same part that came in your set. Its core categories are hand-drive sockets and ratchets, impact sockets, wrenches, screwdrivers and pliers, plus storage from small holders to full cabinets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most important of all, Tekton is a brand that specifies and stands behind its own tools rather than a retailer's house label. Husky belongs to Home Depot and Icon belongs to Harbor Freight; Tekton answers only to itself. That gives it direct control over specification, quality checks and warranty, which is a large part of why our <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">full Tekton tools review</a> rates it so highly for the money. It also means everything below, from the origin breakdown to the warranty terms, comes from Tekton's own published pages rather than from a store policy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-made" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where Tekton Tools Are Actually Made
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton publishes the breakdown itself on its About Us page. About 82 percent of its current product lineup is made in Taiwan, 12 percent in the United States, 3.5 percent in Germany and 2 percent in Canada. The remaining half a percent is 20 items in total: 14 from Sweden, 5 from Poland and 1 from Slovakia. Few tool brands at this price publish anything like that level of detail, and it is worth reading closely rather than skimming.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Tekton's Published Origin Breakdown</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">82%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Made in Taiwan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">12%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Made in the USA</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3.5%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Made in Germany</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Made in Canada</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those figures are shares of the product lineup, not of units sold or dollars spent. A single socket size counts as one item, the same as a rolling cabinet. So the share of Taiwanese steel in your own box depends entirely on what you bought. Do not assume by category either: Tekton makes some wrenches in-house in Michigan, while other wrenches in the catalog come from overseas partners. The only reliable answer for a given tool is the origin listed for that exact item, which the next section shows you how to find.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Michigan side is real manufacturing, not just a warehouse. Tekton says that in its own facilities it cuts, engraves and polishes steel, molds plastic, and carries out a range of finishing and assembly work. The steel for all of its in-house production of wrenches, picks, hooks and pry bars is U.S.-sourced. It also works with outside partners in the United States and abroad. When Tekton labels a tool Made in the USA, it says that means all of the manufacturing happens in the United States, and that it uses U.S. materials whenever possible. That lines up with the Federal Trade Commission's standard that an unqualified Made in USA claim requires the product to be all or virtually all made here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice what is missing from the list: China. Plenty of reviews and forum threads, especially older ones, describe Tekton as made in Taiwan and China, and that may well have been accurate for earlier products. Tekton's current published breakdown does not list China at all. We could not find a Tekton statement on when or whether Chinese-made items left the lineup, so if the distinction matters to you, check the marking on the specific item rather than trusting a review written several product generations ago.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat the numbers as a snapshot, too. Tekton frames them as its current product lineup and talks about growing U.S. manufacturing, and earlier coverage quoted slightly different shares, such as 81.5 percent Taiwan and 4 percent Germany. The direction of travel has been toward more domestic production, but Taiwan remains, by a wide margin, where most Tekton tools come from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="check-origin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Check the Origin of Any Tekton Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            The quickest check is the product page. Every tool on Tekton.com has a Specifications section at the bottom of the page, and country of origin is one of the listed fields. Tekton points buyers there directly, which is unusual; most brands make you hunt for it on the packaging after the tool has arrived. If you are comparing two near-identical items and origin is your tiebreaker, open both pages and read that line before you add anything to the cart.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second check is the tool itself. U.S. customs rules require imported goods to carry a country-of-origin marking, and on hand tools it is usually stamped, forged or laser-etched into the body, or printed on the packaging where the tool is too small to mark. A combination wrench will normally show it on the beam, a socket on its outer wall, and a screwdriver on the shank or handle. If the marking on the tool you received disagrees with the listing you bought from, believe the tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sets need a little more care. Because Tekton assembles its sets from the same individual components it sells separately, the spec line for the set tells you the headline origin, while the component pages tell you about each part. If you are building a mostly American-made kit, look at the individual items you actually use most, typically wrenches, pliers and screwdrivers, rather than at a 100-piece set as a whole.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where you buy matters as well. Tekton's own FAQ says that Amazon and other sellers carry legitimate tools but may be selling an older model, and that some sellers offer used or fake Tekton tools. An older model may have been made somewhere different from today's version, so if origin is a reason you are buying, Tekton.com is the safe source. Bought elsewhere and it is not what you expected? Unwanted tools go back to the retailer you bought from; Tekton's warranty is for tools that fail, not for returns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Taiwan-Made Means for Quality
          </h2>

          <p>
            Taiwan is one of the world's major hand tool exporters, with an industry concentrated largely in central Taiwan around Taichung that has supplied brands in the United States, Europe and Japan for decades. Contract manufacturing is the normal model: a brand designs and specifies the tool, and an established factory forges, machines, heat-treats and finishes it. The same factories frequently build for more than one brand, which is why two tools from different labels can look almost identical. We are not naming any factory here, because brands rarely confirm who builds what and guesses spread quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            What separates two Taiwanese sockets is the specification the brand orders and how strictly it checks the parts that come back. That covers the steel grade and its heat treatment, the tolerance of the broached opening, wall thickness, plating quality and the markings. Steel choice matters most for safety: chrome vanadium is right for hand sockets and chrome molybdenum for impact use, which our guide to <a href="/chrome-vanadium-vs-chrome-molybdenum-sockets/">chrome vanadium vs chrome molybdenum sockets</a> explains. Geometry matters for grip, and a 6-point socket holds a stubborn fastener better than a 12-point, as covered in <a href="/6-point-vs-12-point-sockets/">6-point vs 12-point sockets</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Country of origin is a weak proxy for any of that. American-made tools range from superb to mediocre, and Taiwanese tools include some of the best-regarded mid-price lines on the market. The more useful questions are who wrote the specification, how consistently the brand enforces it, and what happens when a tool fails. With Tekton, the specification, quality control and warranty all sit with the same small company in Michigan, and the warranty, covered next, is the backstop if a bad part gets through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where premium brands still earn their prices is in features rather than in where the steel is cut. Snap-on's proprietary wrench geometry, finer ratchet mechanisms and the convenience of a van that comes to your shop are real advantages, weighed in our <a href="/tekton-vs-snap-on/">Tekton vs Snap-on comparison</a>. GearWrench, which also sources most of its line from Asia, wins on ratcheting wrenches, as our <a href="/tekton-vs-gearwrench/">Tekton vs GearWrench breakdown</a> shows. And Harbor Freight's Icon line is the closest like-for-like rival on price and finish, which we cover in <a href="/tekton-vs-icon/">Tekton vs Icon</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a home mechanic or a tradesperson who is not in a shop eight hours a day, those differences are mostly about feel and convenience rather than about tools breaking. That is why we are comfortable recommending a Taiwan-made Tekton set on its merits, and why the warranty terms deserve as much attention as the country stamped on the steel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Tekton Warranty, Clause by Clause
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton's warranty fits in one sentence on its own page: if your tool does not work like it should, let us know and we will make it right. The three supporting promises are no time limits, no receipts, and shipping on Tekton. It does not matter when you got the tool or how old it is, and when Tekton sends a replacement it always pays the shipping on the new item.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who is covered is broader than with most brands. Tekton's wording is that if you own the tool, its support comes with it, and its FAQ adds that it warranties its products regardless of where or when you bought them. Most competitors limit coverage to the original purchaser. As written, Tekton's policy covers a wrench you inherited or bought secondhand, as long as it is a genuine Tekton tool.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If it is a genuine Tekton and it stopped working properly, file the claim.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">No receipt, no time limit, and in most cases no need to send the broken tool back. Photograph it with your name and the date, include the item number, and claims received before 2 p.m. Eastern on a weekday can ship the same day.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The claim itself runs through the warranty form on Tekton.com. You upload photos of the damaged tool with your name and the date visible in the picture, usually written on a slip of paper beside it, plus the item number and your shipping address, and you can add a note on what went wrong. Tekton says that if it receives a complete request before 2 p.m. Eastern on a weekday, it ships the replacement the same day. In most cases it does not ask you to return the broken tool, and phone support runs 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Eastern, Monday to Friday.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two details are easy to miss. First, if your tool has been discontinued and there is no direct replacement, Tekton says it will issue a credit greater than the last list price of that tool to spend on Tekton.com, so an old pattern of ratchet does not leave you stranded. Second, replacements ship within the United States and Canada only. Tekton offers what it calls limited warranty support outside those two countries, so buyers elsewhere should not count on the same experience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Owner reports on forums broadly match the policy: quick replacements, few questions, and no fuss over age. That said, this is a discretionary policy run by one company, not a legal guarantee with a long list of defined terms. It has been consistent for years, but the honest way to value it is as a strong, well-run policy that could change, not as an insurance contract.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What the Warranty Does Not Cover
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton does not publish a list of exclusions. The test is simply whether the tool works like it should, which leaves the judgment with Tekton. Most of the industry explicitly excludes abuse, such as a cheater bar on a ratchet, a wrench used as a hammer, or a chrome hand socket run on an impact gun. Owners report Tekton being lenient, but you should not plan on the warranty covering a tool you broke by using it for something it was never designed to do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Torque wrenches are the clearest boundary. Tekton runs a separate, paid recertification service, and its form states that it is not for warranty claims. At the time of writing, recertification costs $50 if your wrench already tests within its stated accuracy and $75 if it needs adjusting, with each additional wrench in the same shipment at $35 or $60. Tekton emails a prepaid shipping label, usually within one business day, and the service typically takes two business days once the wrench arrives. Only the micrometer, split-beam and electronic models listed on the form are eligible.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical reading is that a torque wrench that breaks or arrives clearly out of spec is a warranty matter, while normal drift after years of use is maintenance you pay for. That is how most brands treat it, and Tekton's published pages do not promise a separate calibration guarantee period. A widely used rule of thumb, drawn from the ISO 6789 standard, is to have a click wrench checked every 12 months or 5,000 cycles, whichever comes first. If you are still choosing a wrench, our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">best torque wrench guide</a> compares accuracy and service options across brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear parts sit in a gray zone. Tekton does not single out driver bits or other consumables, while GearWrench, for example, explicitly lists accessory bits, bit sockets, taps and dies as consumables with no warranty at all. A bit with a twisted tip has arguably done its job rather than failed, so expect a judgment call rather than an automatic swap. A ratchet that slips under load or a socket that cracks in normal use is exactly what the warranty is for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the warranty is not a return policy. If you simply bought the wrong size or changed your mind, the tool goes back to whoever sold it to you, under that seller's return window. And a counterfeit bought from a marketplace seller is not a Tekton tool, so it has no Tekton warranty, which is one more reason to buy from Tekton.com or a known dealer.
          </p>
        </div>

