        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading Grand Rapids as Made in USA</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tekton is a Michigan company and does real manufacturing there, but by its own count only about 12 percent of the lineup is made in the United States. If domestic manufacture is the reason you are buying, check the origin line in each product's specifications instead of assuming from the address on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting an Old Review on Origin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plenty of older reviews and forum posts say Taiwan and China. Tekton's current published breakdown lists no China at all, and the mix keeps shifting toward U.S. production. A review written several product generations ago is a poor guide; the marking on the tool you hold and today's product page are the reliable sources.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the Warranty to Cover Calibration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A torque wrench that breaks is a warranty claim. One that has simply drifted after years of use is maintenance, and Tekton handles it through a paid recertification service at $50 to $75 per wrench at the time of writing. Budget for a periodic check rather than expecting free recalibration for life.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Name-and-Date Photo</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tekton does not ask for a receipt, but it does ask for photos of the damaged tool with your name and the date visible. Leave that out, or forget the item number, and the claim stalls while support asks for it. Get it right before 2 p.m. Eastern on a weekday and the replacement can ship the same day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Suspiciously Cheap Tekton Set</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tekton warns that some marketplace sellers offer used or fake Tekton tools, and that other sellers may be selling older models. A counterfeit has no Tekton warranty and may not match the origin you expect. Tekton rarely runs sales, so a price far below Tekton.com is a reason for caution rather than a bargain.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Tekton tools made in China?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not according to Tekton's current published breakdown, which lists Taiwan at about 82 percent, the USA at 12 percent, Germany at 3.5 percent, Canada at 2 percent and a handful of items from Sweden, Poland and Slovakia. Older reviews often mention China, which may reflect earlier products. Check the origin line on the product page or the marking on the tool itself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are any Tekton tools made in the USA?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. About 12 percent of the current lineup is made in the United States, and Tekton cuts, engraves and polishes steel, molds plastic and assembles tools in its own Michigan facilities. The steel for its in-house wrenches, picks, hooks and pry bars is U.S.-sourced. Each product page lists its country of origin under Specifications.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a receipt for a Tekton warranty claim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most cases, no. Tekton's policy is no time limits and no receipts. Instead it usually asks for a photo of the broken tool with your name and the date visible, along with the item number and your shipping address. Replacements ship free within the United States and Canada, often the same day for complete claims made before 2 p.m. Eastern.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the warranty cover Tekton tools bought on Amazon or secondhand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Tekton says it warranties its products regardless of where or when you bought them, and that if you own the tool, its support comes with it. That covers genuine Tekton tools bought from Amazon, a dealer or secondhand. It does not cover counterfeits, which Tekton warns some marketplace sellers offer, so buy from Tekton.com or a known dealer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to send the broken tool back to Tekton?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Tekton's FAQ says that in most cases it does not need you to return the product, only an image of the broken tool. Torque wrench recertification is different: that is a paid service where Tekton emails a prepaid label and you ship the wrench in, with service typically taking two business days after it arrives.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Tekton warranty cover torque wrench calibration?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not as routine maintenance. A wrench that fails is a warranty claim, but recalibration runs through a separate recertification service: $50 if the wrench is already within spec and $75 if it needs adjustment, at the time of writing. Most brands treat calibration the same way. A common rule is a check every 12 months or 5,000 cycles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Tekton a good brand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home mechanics and many tradespeople, yes. The tools are built mainly in Taiwan to Tekton's own specifications, the company publishes its origin data openly, and the warranty is unusually simple, with no receipt and no time limit. Choose a premium or store brand instead if you need instant in-person replacement or specialist features.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
